## Alignment services for heavy-duty vehicles and commercial fleets

- Front-end alignment
- Rear axle alignment
- Four-wheel alignment
- Alignment adjustments after suspension repairs
- Tire alignment inspections
- Caster and camber adjustments
- Toe adjustments

## Why bring it here

Charlotte has plenty of shops that will align a car. There are very few that can
put a tractor on a rack and measure the drive axles against the chassis
centerline, and that measurement — the thrust angle — is the one that decides
whether the whole combination rolls straight or drags sideways down the
interstate.

We measure the foundation before we touch the geometry: ride height, kingpins,
tie-rod ends, bushings and shocks first, then toe, camber, caster and thrust
angle. An alignment set on a worn front end is a number that will not hold.

{% specs label="What gets measured" rows=[
  {label: "Steer axle", value: "Toe, camber, caster", note: "sets tire wear and steering feel"},
  {label: "Drive axles", value: "Thrust angle", note: "squareness to the chassis centerline"},
  {label: "Trailer tandems", value: "Parallelism", note: "measured to the kingpin"},
  {label: "Checked first", value: "Ride height", note: "geometry read on a wrong ride height is a wrong reading"}
] /%}

The money side of this is in [the real ROI of a fleet alignment
program](/articles/how-misalignment-eats-tires-fuel-and-uptime-the-real-roi-of-a-fleet-alignment-program).

